# What this file is
# ----------------
# A Caddyfile fragment, imported inside the site block by
# deploy/caddy/Caddyfile.coexist and by dev/Caddyfile.dev. For every route
# group that has at least one Rust-owned route it defines
#
#   @plane_rs_<group>  a named matcher: exact path(s) and exact method(s)
#   handle @plane_rs_<group> { reverse_proxy <Rust upstream> }
#
# `handle` is used instead of a bare `reverse_proxy` because handle blocks
# are mutually exclusive and are not reordered by the Caddyfile adapter.
# Both Caddyfiles that import this fragment wrap their production routing in
# one fallback `handle`, in the same mutually-exclusive group, so a Rust
# handle that matches ends routing there: the fallback is skipped and the
# request is proxied exactly once. A bare `reverse_proxy` with a named
# matcher would be sorted after the single-path directives (Caddy sorts
# same-named directives by path-matcher specificity) and would let the
# request continue down the chain, reaching Django as well.
#
# Path matching
# -------------
# Patterns are anchored ^...$ RE2 regexes built from the Django converter
# regexes (see DJANGO_CONVERTERS in gen_caddy.py; uuid is lowercase-only),
# DRF's `.format` suffix regex, and DRF's optional trailing slash. Caddy's
# path_regexp matches the URI-decoded, cleaned path (it runs against
# r.URL.Path and merges doubled slashes / resolves dot segments); Django's
# resolver also works on the decoded PATH_INFO, so the two agree. The
# cleaning means a request like /api//instances/ is matched here as
# /api/instances/ where Django would 404; Rust must 404 such paths itself
# (its router will not have them).
#
# Method matching
# ---------------
# A whole-row `rust` includes the implicit HEAD Django answers for a GET
# and the OPTIONS DRF answers for every DRF view; `rust(GET,HEAD)` moves
# only the listed methods and leaves the rest of the row on Django.
# Clusters are OR-ed as (method(...) && path_regexp(...)), so a path that is
# Rust-owned for GET and Django-owned for POST is split correctly even when
# other routes in the same group have a different method set.
#
# Flags
# -----
# Each group is controlled by PLANE_RS_GROUP_<NAME>. The value is read per
# request with the {env.*} placeholder, not substituted at config load with
# {$...}: the generated file is therefore identical in every environment,
# and a deployment flips a group by setting the variable and restarting (or
# reloading) the proxy. `rust` -- or unset -- sends the group to Rust;
# `django` (or any other value) sends every method back to Django. A group
# with no Rust-owned route has no matcher, so its flag has no effect until
# a route in it is marked rust.
#
# Rust upstream: {$PLANE_RS_API_UPSTREAM:plane-api:8000} (env PLANE_RS_API_UPSTREAM,
# overridden by Caddyfile.dev to reach the host).
